{"OptimizedCursorPaginator is a newly introduced class with no other references in the codebase. It contains fundamental issues (datetime handling, negative slicing) that indicate it hasn't been adequately tested. Consider using the established DateTimePaginator or thoroughly vetting this new paginator before use.": 0.8593852519989014, "Django QuerySets do not support negative indexing \u2014 queryset[negative_offset:stop] raises AssertionError: Negative indexing is not supported. The comment claiming Django handles this automatically is incorrect. Any call with enable_advanced_features=True and a negative cursor.offset will crash at runtime.": 0.9879658818244934, "organization_context.member can be None (per its type annotation and docstring in RpcUserOrganizationContext). Accessing .has_global_access on None will raise an AttributeError at runtime for users without org membership.": 0.9705771207809448, "OptimizedCursorPaginator.get_item_key() applies math.floor()/math.ceil() directly to the raw attribute value. With order_by=-datetime, this value is a datetime object, which will raise TypeError. The existing DateTimePaginator avoids this by converting to a float timestamp first via strftime.": 0.9782865047454834, "Misleading comments: no code was actually changed, yet the comments claim negative offsets are now supported as a performance optimization. The existing pagination logic (_build_next_values, _build_prev_values) does not handle negative offsets correctly \u2014 e.g., offset + limit would produce wrong page boundaries, and bool(value or offset) would incorrectly signal a previous page exists.": 0.8101649284362793}